INT. HARBOR FERRY TERMINAL - WAITING ROOM - DAY

Plastic chairs bolted in rows. A vending machine hums. Through the window, the ferry rocks at the dock. At a folding table in the corner, a deposition.

RUTH KOWALCZYK, 50s, attorney, reading glasses on a chain. ARJUN BOSE, 40s, deponent, anxious, tie too tight. LINNEA STRAND, 30s, court reporter, fingers poised over the stenotype.

RUTH
(to the room)
Let the record show we are in the Harbor terminal because the courthouse flooded.

LINNEA
Noted.

RUTH
Mr. Bose, state your name.

ARJUN
Arjun Bose.

RUTH
And your occupation?

ARJUN
Dock inspector.

RUTH
Were you on duty on the fourteenth of March?

ARJUN
I was.

RUTH
Did you sign the crane certificate that morning?

ARJUN
I... may have.

RUTH
May have?

ARJUN
Did.

The vending machine clunks. A bag of chips falls.

RUTH
(muttering)
Even the snacks have a better memory than my witness.

ARJUN
I'm sorry?

RUTH
Nothing. Next question. Mr. Bose, did you inspect the crane before signing?

ARJUN
I inspected a crane.

RUTH
Was it the crane in question?

ARJUN
It was a crane.

LINNEA
(quietly)
Counsel, I need a clear answer for the record.

RUTH
Mr. Bose, it's either yes or no.

ARJUN
No.

RUTH
No, you did not inspect the correct crane?

ARJUN
No, I did not.

LINNEA
Could you both speak one at a time?

RUTH
Mr. Bose, who told you to sign?

ARJUN
My supervisor.

ARJUN
My supervisor said it was routine.

RUTH ^
Your supervisor is a Mr. Dillard.

ARJUN
Yes.

RUTH
(to the room)
Let the record show the witness has just changed his testimony.

The ferry horn sounds. All three look up. Linnea types. Arjun loosens his tie.
